  "reasoning": "The patient presents with chronic chest pain ('hurting for a while'), nausea, and significant anxiety. These symptoms strongly align with gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD), which is characterized by persistent chest discomfort (heartburn/regurgitation), nausea, and can trigger or worsen anxiety due to the distressing nature of the symptoms. While shivering is mentioned, it can be an autonomic response to prolonged pain or discomfort. Other candidates were considered but ruled out: peptic ulcer disease typically presents with epigastric/stomach pain rather than chest pain; allergies usually involve respiratory or cutaneous symptoms like throat swelling or rashes; hypertension often presents with headaches and dizziness rather than prominent chest pain and nausea; and infections like pneumonia or common cold would feature prominent coughing or fever. The symptom cluster best matches the clinical presentation of GERD.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]gastroesophageal reflux disease[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}
